``` Gentlemen Ransomware Affiliate Azazel: CI/CD Secret Theft, LEAKNED Extortion and MCP Command Execution
    Field names follow CIM/UDM conventions and have not been tested against live data; adjust them to your environment.
    Splunk hunt, CIM data models (tstats) plus raw log searches for behaviours. Author: daniyyell-dev. https://github.com/daniyyell-dev/rapid-threat-intel/tree/main/threats/2026-11_November-2026/2026-11-11/Gentlemen-Affiliate-Azazel-LEAKNED-CICD-Secret-Theft
    Sources: https://www.cloudsek.com/blog/caught-in-4k-the-gentlemen-files ; https://www.bitdefender.com/en-us/blog/businessinsights/bitdefender-threat-debrief-november-2026
    Published 2026-10-05. Set the time picker to at least the last 30 days, or back to the earliest activity in the sources.
    The indicators below are live and malicious: do not browse to them.
    Needs these CIM data models populated: Network_Traffic, Network_Resolution, Web, Email, Endpoint. A model with no data simply returns nothing.
    summariesonly=false also reads events the acceleration has not summarised yet. If these data models are
    accelerated, summariesonly=true is much faster but only sees the summary range.
    No CIM? Use hunt_raw.spl with the rti_* macros from all/splunk_macros.conf.
    Output: first_seen, last_seen, ioc, ioc_type, data_model, host, user, src, dest, detail, count

    Behaviours (indicators of attack) are extra sections below, with ioc_type "behaviour (alert)" or "behaviour (hunt)".
    Behaviours are leads, not confirmed indicators, and can match legitimate activity. Compare any hit against your own baseline and environment before acting.
    Behaviours are for hunting and alerting only; never block on them.
    Sections that read raw logs use index=*: replace it with your own index names to make them faster. ```
| tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
    FROM datamodel=Network_Traffic.All_Traffic
    WHERE All_Traffic.dest_ip IN ("23.236.169.183", "162.220.163.26", "66.179.30.155", "141.95.252.30", "66.203.124.135")
    BY All_Traffic.src_ip All_Traffic.dest_ip All_Traffic.dest_port All_Traffic.action All_Traffic.dvc All_Traffic.user
| rename "All_Traffic.*" AS *
| eval data_model="Network_Traffic", ioc_type="ipv4", ioc=dest_ip, host=dvc, src=src_ip, dest=dest_ip, detail="connection to IOC, port ".dest_port.", ".action
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
        FROM datamodel=Network_Traffic.All_Traffic
        WHERE All_Traffic.src_ip IN ("23.236.169.183", "162.220.163.26", "66.179.30.155", "141.95.252.30", "66.203.124.135")
        BY All_Traffic.src_ip All_Traffic.dest_ip All_Traffic.dest_port All_Traffic.action All_Traffic.dvc All_Traffic.user
    | rename "All_Traffic.*" AS *
    | eval data_model="Network_Traffic", ioc_type="ipv4", ioc=src_ip, host=dvc, src=src_ip, dest=dest_ip, detail="connection from IOC, port ".dest_port.", ".action
]
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
        FROM datamodel=Network_Resolution.DNS
        WHERE DNS.query IN ("forgitlab.com", "*.forgitlab.com")
        BY DNS.src DNS.dest DNS.query DNS.reply_code
    | rename "DNS.*" AS *
    | rex field=query "(?i)(?<ioc>(?:forgitlab\.com))\.?$"
    | eval data_model="Network_Resolution", ioc_type="domain", host=src, user="-", detail="DNS query ".query." (".reply_code.")"
]
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
        FROM datamodel=Web.Web
        WHERE (Web.url IN ("*://forgitlab.com*", "*.forgitlab.com*", "forgitlab.com*") OR Web.dest IN ("forgitlab.com", "*.forgitlab.com"))
        BY Web.src Web.user Web.dest Web.url Web.http_user_agent Web.status
    | rename "Web.*" AS *
    | rex field=url "(?i)(?<ioc>forgitlab\.com)"
    | eval ioc=coalesce(ioc, dest), data_model="Web", ioc_type=if(match(ioc, "/"), "url", "domain"), host=src, detail="HTTP ".status." ".url." UA: ".http_user_agent
]
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
        FROM datamodel=Email.All_Email
        WHERE (All_Email.url IN ("*://forgitlab.com*", "*.forgitlab.com*", "forgitlab.com*"))
        BY All_Email.src_user All_Email.recipient All_Email.subject All_Email.file_name All_Email.file_hash All_Email.url
    | rename "All_Email.*" AS *
    | eval matched=file_hash." ".url
    | rex field=matched "(?i)(?<ioc>forgitlab\.com)"
    | eval data_model="Email", ioc_type=case(match(ioc, "^[0-9a-fA-F]{64}$"), "sha256", match(ioc, "^[0-9a-fA-F]{40}$"), "sha1", match(ioc, "^[0-9a-fA-F]{32}$"), "md5", match(ioc, "/"), "url", 1==1, "domain"), host="-", user=recipient, src=src_user, dest=recipient, detail="email '".subject."' ".file_name
]

``` Behaviour 1 (Alert): Object storage mirrored out with the MinIO client
    Look for: mc mirror (MinIO Client) copying a bucket to a remote destination you do not own
    Log source: Linux process events, MinIO audit logs
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
        FROM datamodel=Endpoint.Processes
        WHERE Processes.process_name IN ("mc", "mc.exe") Processes.process="*mirror*" NOT Processes.process="*localhost*"
        BY Processes.dest Processes.user Processes.parent_process_name Processes.process_name Processes.process
    | rename "Processes.*" AS *
    | eval host=dest, src="-", detail=parent_process_name." > ".process, data_model="Endpoint.Processes"
    | eval ioc="Object storage mirrored out with the MinIO client", ioc_type="behaviour (alert)"
]

``` Behaviour 3 (Alert): Ransom notes planted on server surfaces
    Look for: Ransom notes written to /etc/motd, the sshd_config Banner, the PostgreSQL cluster_name, the pgAdmin login template, a GitLab README or a new GitLab issue
    Log source: Linux file integrity monitoring, GitLab audit events, PostgreSQL logs
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
        FROM datamodel=Endpoint.Filesystem
        WHERE Filesystem.file_name="ATTENTION_SENSITIVE_INFORMATION.txt"
        BY Filesystem.dest Filesystem.user Filesystem.file_path Filesystem.file_name Filesystem.action
    | rename "Filesystem.*" AS *
    | eval host=dest, src="-", detail="file ".action." ".file_path, data_model="Endpoint.Filesystem"
    | eval ioc="Ransom notes planted on server surfaces", ioc_type="behaviour (alert)"
]

``` Behaviour 5 (Hunt): Exposed MCP server or MCP scanner clients
    Look for: MCP server listening beyond loopback, or MCP initialize requests from clients named internet-census-mcp-scanner or hermes
    Log source: MCP server logs, reverse proxy logs, network listeners
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    search index=* ("internet-census-mcp-scanner" OR "exec_in_session")
    | eval host=host, user=coalesce(user, "-"), src=coalesce(src, src_ip, "-"), dest=sourcetype, detail=substr(replace(_raw, "\s+", " "), 1, 300)
    | fillnull value="-" host user src dest detail
    | stats count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen BY host user src dest detail
    | eval data_model="MCP and proxy logs"
    | eval ioc="Exposed MCP server or MCP scanner clients", ioc_type="behaviour (hunt)"
]

``` Behaviour 6 (Hunt): Large uploads to MEGA or to port 9999
    Look for: Large uploads from servers to MEGA (mega-cmd-server) or HTTP POSTs to port 9999 on unfamiliar hosts
    Log source: Firewall, proxy and NetFlow logs, EDR network events
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen sum(All_Traffic.bytes_out) AS bytes_out
        FROM datamodel=Network_Traffic.All_Traffic
        WHERE All_Traffic.dest_port=9999
        BY All_Traffic.src_ip All_Traffic.dest_ip All_Traffic.dvc
    | rename "All_Traffic.*" AS *
    | eval host=dvc, user="-", src=src_ip, dest=dest_ip, detail="port 9999, ".round(bytes_out/1048576, 1)." MB out", data_model="Network_Traffic"
    ``` 10 MB threshold: adjust to your baseline ```
    | where bytes_out>=10485760
    | fields - src_ip dest_ip dvc bytes_out
    | eval ioc="Large uploads to MEGA or to port 9999", ioc_type="behaviour (hunt)"
]
| append [
    | tstats summariesonly=false fillnull_value="-" count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
        FROM datamodel=Endpoint.Processes
        WHERE Processes.process_name IN ("mega-cmd-server", "MEGAcmdServer.exe", "mega-cmd")
        BY Processes.dest Processes.user Processes.parent_process_name Processes.process_name Processes.process
    | rename "Processes.*" AS *
    | eval host=dest, src="-", detail=parent_process_name." > ".process, data_model="Endpoint.Processes"
    | eval ioc="Large uploads to MEGA or to port 9999", ioc_type="behaviour (hunt)"
]
| sort 0 - last_seen
| eval first_seen=strftime(first_seen, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"), last_seen=strftime(last_seen, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
| table first_seen last_seen ioc ioc_type data_model host user src dest detail count

``` Behaviours to check by hand (no reliable Splunk field mapping, so no search is written):

    Behaviour 2 (Hunt): Bulk reads of GitLab CI/CD secrets
        Look for: Bulk reads of GitLab CI/CD variables, project exports or repository clones by a token from an unfamiliar address
        Log source: GitLab audit events and API logs
        Why no search: GitLab audit event names depend on edition and version; review variable reads, project exports and clones per token against its normal use

    Behaviour 4 (Alert): PostgreSQL killed and wiped after exfiltration
        Look for: PostgreSQL process killed and its data directory deleted shortly after large pg_dump or scp transfers
        Log source: Linux process events, PostgreSQL logs
        Why no search: a sequence (large pg_dump or scp, then the PostgreSQL process stopped and its data directory deleted); review process and file events on database hosts after any large transfer

    Behaviours are leads, not confirmed indicators, and can match legitimate activity. Compare any hit against your own baseline and environment before acting. Behaviours are for hunting and alerting only; never block on them. ```
``` Other hunt leads (not network or hash indicators), check manually:
    [filename] ATTENTION_SENSITIVE_INFORMATION.txt
    [other] internet-census-mcp-scanner
    [other] hermes ```
``` Behavioural hunt notes:
    mc mirror, aws s3 sync and pg_dump are normal admin tools, so judge hits on the destination: an address or bucket you do not own is the signal.
    In GitLab audit and API logs, look for bulk reads of CI/CD variables, project exports or repository clones by a token from an unfamiliar address, and for README commits or new issues made with a pipeline token.
    If you run MCP servers, review their logs for initialize requests from clients named internet-census-mcp-scanner or hermes and for any listener bound beyond 127.0.0.1. ```
